{"type":"result","subtype":"success","is_error":false,"duration_ms":325703,"duration_api_ms":417797,"num_turns":30,"result":"","stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"5dc8c22a-5506-4516-a401-d16ebf42e31b","total_cost_usd":1.7243802499999998,"usage":{"input_tokens":5288,"cache_creation_input_tokens":74455,"cache_read_input_tokens":1190194,"output_tokens":11406,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":74455,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-opus-4-6":{"inputTokens":5288,"outputTokens":11406,"cacheReadInputTokens":1190194,"cacheCreationInputTokens":74455,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":1.37203075,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":64000},"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":4190,"outputTokens":13127,"cacheReadInputTokens":1409395,"cacheCreationInputTokens":113268,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.3523495000000001,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000}},"permission_denials":[],"structured_output":{"amendments":[{"body":"Add an invariant check in `enforce_invariants` (invariants.rs) that validates `sum(egg_cohorts[*].count) == berried_females_count`. Currently `clamp_berried_to_adults` (line 42) and `retain(|c| c.count > 0)` (line 44) fix secondary invariants but never verify the primary one: that the per-cohort egg counts sum to the berried-female count. If these diverge (through save corruption, a future code change, or the legacy migration path at shrimp.rs:465-476 creating a cohort without matching counts), orphaned egg cohorts can silently resolve and produce phantom juveniles that inflate population. The fix is a one-line assertion or repair after line 44 in enforce_invariants.","rationale":"This is the only structural invariant in the berried/egg-cohort bookkeeping that is not validated. All mutation sites (spawning, egg_development, clamp_berried_to_adults) maintain the relationship, but nothing enforces it post-hoc. A single future change that touches only one side would silently break population conservation."},{"body":"Cap `maturation_accum` on each StageCohort so it cannot exceed the stage's `count`. In `juvenile_to_subadult` (shrimp.rs:641) and `subadult_to_adult` (shrimp.rs:700), unfunded candidates are added back to `maturation_accum` without bound. If reserves are chronically insufficient, the accumulator grows by `count * daily_rate` per tick indefinitely. When reserves finally recover, the entire cohort promotes in a single tick (bounded by count, so correct), but the unbounded float accumulation wastes precision and makes the state harder to reason about. Add a clamp like `maturation_accum = maturation_accum.min(count as f64)` after the unfunded re-addition, or in `enforce_invariants`.","rationale":"While not a crash or conservation bug (promotions are always capped at count), an unbounded float accumulator is a latent robustness risk. Clamping it costs nothing and makes the invariant explicit: maturation progress can never exceed one promotion per individual."},{"body":"Add a test that explicitly checks population count conservation across a multi-day run: `total_count_change == total_births - total_deaths`. The existing tests verify conditions, events, and individual stage counts, but no test asserts the fundamental accounting identity that population change equals births minus deaths. This is the central invariant of the stage model per the acceptance criteria ('counts conserved except for explicit births and deaths'). A test could track `juvenile.count` additions from hatching vs `total_count()` delta and mortality counts over a 30-day run.","rationale":"Count conservation is the core contract of the stage-structured model. Without a dedicated test, a future refactor could silently break the accounting (e.g., by adding to one stage without subtracting from another during transitions) and only be caught much later through symptom-level failures."}],"summary":"Thorough review of the stage-structured shrimp population implementation across shrimp.rs, biology.rs, save.rs, snapshot.rs, invariants.rs, and engine.rs. The implementation is solid overall: four lifecycle stages (juvenile, sub-adult, adult, berried) with per-stage reserve/condition, deterministic forward transitions via carry-forward accumulators, proper reserve transfer on promotion and death, stage-aware feeding/mortality/reproduction, correct save migration (v4→v5) preserving counts exactly, and snapshot/API/TUI exposing both stage breakdown and backward-compatible totals. Three amendments proposed: (1) missing invariant check for berried_females_count vs egg_cohort sum, (2) unbounded maturation accumulator when reserves are insufficient, (3) no explicit count-conservation test despite it being a core acceptance criterion."},"fast_mode_state":"off","uuid":"8b6b0990-0d02-42ea-848b-a5fae9338ba6"}
